  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 331 PM EST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nSynoptic setup this afternoon consists of deep troughing across the \nwestern CONUS, while a large ridge continues to encompass the \neastern two-thirds of the country. So, it has been another \nquiet day across the U.P. with little cloud cover per latest vis\nsatellite imagery and plenty of sunshine. Ground-based obs \nacross the area are reporting widespread high temperatures in \nthe mid to upper 50s. This prolonged period of warm \nsouthwesterly flow will persist through Saturday as a surface \nlow in association with the aforementioned trough tracks \nnortheastward from the Rockies and eventually through southern \nOntario by Thursday. Daytime highs in the mid to upper 50s will \npush record levels, especially on Thursday and Saturday. Record \nhighs at WFO MQT are 53F on Thursday (1963) and 56F on Saturday \n(1990). Meanwhile, the forecast continues to trend dry until the\nweekend with an especially windy day Thursday. Widespread \nsouthwesterly wind gusts of 25 to 30 mph can be expected across \nthe majority of the U.P. with the Keweenaw even topping off in \nexcess of 40 mph. \n\nFurther out, focus for the next chance of precipitation remains on \nSaturday as another trough enters the region. Moisture advection \nahead of this one looks more favorable for measurable precipitation \nover the weekend. But, with the U.P. initially in the warm sector of \nits associated surface low, p-type will likely be in the form of \nrain until cold air advection on the backside can overspread the \nforecast area Saturday night. Lake effect snow as flow becomes \nnorthwesterly will need the most monitoring, but the potential for \nany significant accumulations remains low at this time. And, high\npressure becomes re-established early next week. Nonetheless, \ncontinue to monitor the forecast for any rapid changes if \ntraveling during the holiday week.",
